# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Once you've created a project and have a working API key, you can use it to access AI models through the Justice AI Gateway.

## Before you use the Justice AI Gateway

The Justice AI Gateway is a secure way to access AI models. The Gateway is managed by the Office of the CTO and maintained by the Justice Data Platform team.
It's designed to help you use AI models safely and securely, while also managing access, costs and usage for the organisation.

In addition to [using the Justice Data Platform responsibly](../get-started/index.html#your-responsibilities-as-a-user), you must also meet the requirements listed below when using the Justice AI Gateway.

### 1. Only use the Gateway for an approved purpose

You can use the Justice AI Gateway for work approved by your business unit with a suitable data protection impact assessment (DPIA) in place.

You must not use it for:

- coding productivity
- personal or non-work purposes
- content which violates Ministry of Justice policies

### 2. Store and manage your API keys securely

Treat your API key like a password. You must store it securely and avoid including it in your application code.

If you think your API key has been exposed or compromised, regenerate it immediately and report the incident to [the #ask-data-platform Slack channel](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0B949G0J2X).

### 3. Understand and monitor project usage limits

Check the [default usage limits per project](../get-started/index.html#fair-usage-and-cost-allocations) before using the Justice AI Gateway.
You should regularly monitor your project's usage to avoid exceeding your allowances, which could result in your requests being blocked.

If you need to exceed the default limits, you can [request an increase to your AI usage limits](/support/#request-an-increase-to-your-ai-usage-limits).

### 4. Set up appropriate error handling and logging

Make sure your application has appropriate error handling and diagnostic logging in place to help you identify and resolve issues with your requests.
Your application should be able to handle failed requests and log relevant information, including:

- HTTP status code
- any error message
- the time of the request

### 5. Use resources proportionately

When using the Justice AI Gateway, make sure you:

- minimise requests and prompt size
- limit outputs to only what you need
- choose the cheapest suitable model for your use case
- reuse outputs where possible, rather than generating new ones

To do this, you should plan and optimise your AI usage before starting work on the Gateway. Plan your requests carefully, and provide clear instructions without unnecessary or repetitive information.
Be mindful of the resources consumed and aim to use AI efficiently without compromising the quality of results.

## Create an API key for your project

After creating your project, follow the steps on the Justice Data Platform to create an API key. Make sure you save the API key in a secure place.

If you need to make API key changes (for example, create a new key or regenerate an existing key), you can find more information in the [Managing projects](/managing-projects/) section of this user guide.

> Do not use Justice AI Gateway API keys for AI-assisted coding tasks, such as code generation or completion. [Request a GitHub Copilot licence](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/.github/blob/main/docs/github-copilot-guidance.md#how-to-request-a-licence) for this instead.

## Check which endpoint you need to use

The Justice AI Gateway has two endpoints:

* an internal endpoint for users connecting from MOJ services
* a standard endpoint for users connecting from other environments

You should use the internal endpoint if you're connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform. You can access it from `https://internal.ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk`.

<!-- You should use the internal endpoint if you're connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform or Modernisation Platform. You can access it from `https://internal.ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk`. -->

Use the standard endpoint if you're connecting from any other environment. You can access it from `https://ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk`.

## Check your API key works

After you’ve created your API key and checked which endpoint to use, make a basic request to the Justice AI Gateway to check it works.
You can do this by completing the steps below.

### Test your API key in your IDE

1. Follow OpenAI’s documentation to [install an official SDK](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/libraries?#install-an-official-sdk) for your language.

1. Set your API key as an environment variable using the following command:

    ```bash
    export AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY="<your-api-key>"
    ```

1. Send an API request in your IDE. You can use the Python example request below, replacing `<model-name>` with the exact name of the model you want to use.
    You should replace the standard endpoint with the internal endpoint if you are connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform.

    ```python
    import os
    from openai import OpenAI

    client = OpenAI(
        api_key=os.environ["AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY"],
        base_url="https://ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk/",
    )

    response = client.responses.create(
        model="<model-name>",
        input="Reply with: Your API key is working",
    )

    print(response.output_text)
    ```

1. Check the response. If successful, you’ll receive `Your API key is working` or a similar response.

### Test your API key with `curl`

If the request in your IDE is successful, you do not need to do this step. You can use it to help identify whether a problem is with your API key or the Justice Data Platform.

1. Set your API key as an environment variable using the following command:

    ```bash
    export AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY="<your-api-key>"
    ```

1. Send a request using `curl`, replacing placeholders in the example below with your API key and the exact name of the model you want to use.
    You should also replace the standard endpoint with the internal endpoint if you are connecting from MOJ's Cloud Platform.

     ```bash
     curl https://ai-gateway.justice.gov.uk/responses \
       -H "Authorization: Bearer $AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY" \
       -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
       -d '{
         "model": "<model-name>",
         "input": "Reply with: Your API key is working"
       }'
    ```

1. Check the response. If successful, you’ll receive `Your API key is working` or a similar response.

## Error handling for API calls

Your code should account for requests that fail and log information to help you identify and resolve issues.

Configure your error handling to log:

* the HTTP status code
* any error message
* the response body (if available)
* the time of the request

> Do not log sensitive data such as API keys.

If the log does not make the cause clear, try the following:

* confirm your API key is correctly set as an environment variable
* check your request specifies the correct model name and includes the version number
* check the [status page](https://status.data-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/posts/dashboard) for any ongoing incidents
* make sure you have not exceeded usage limits (select your project in the Justice Data Platform, then go to *Usage*)

Raise an issue in [the #ask-data-platform Slack channel](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0B949G0J2X) if the problem continues, including your Project ID and Key ID (not your API key). You can find these by going to *Projects* in the Justice Data Platform and selecting your project.

### How AI safeguards affect your requests

As part of our security policy, we use automated checks ('guardrails') to safeguard the Justice AI Gateway from malicious or harmful prompts.
If your API request is blocked by these checks, the API returns a `400 Bad Request` response with a message referring to the AI Gateway security policy.

You should first try simplifying or rewording your prompt if it's blocked.

If the problem continues, you can raise an issue in [the #ask-data-platform Slack channel](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0B949G0J2X) with the full error response.
Our team can investigate platform issues, but the Justice AI Gateway does not retain prompt content, so we cannot determine why a specific prompt was blocked.

